POST Object bypasses conditional write bucket policies
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/c7a9d576e19881918edfe24a8cbbe91f?repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin&sev=
Criticality: high (attack path: high)
Status: invalid

# Resolution
Reviewed against AWS on 03/05/2026. The reported security issue is invalid for
AWS compatibility: POST Object does not populate `s3:ObjectCreationOperation` in
bucket-policy evaluation, so a Deny guarded by
`Bool: { "s3:ObjectCreationOperation": "true" }` is not expected to apply to
POST Object. The proposed patch in this report, which set
`ObjectCreationOperation=true` for POST Object, would have made local behavior
less AWS-compatible.

The review did expose a related conformance nuance. AWS does expose POST Object
HTTP `If-Match` and `If-None-Match` headers to bucket-policy condition keys
`s3:if-match` and `s3:if-none-match`, including exact `StringEquals` matching,
but those headers are policy context only for POST Object. They do not enforce
normal write preconditions: repeated POST Object requests with
`If-None-Match: *` or a matching `If-Match` still return 204 and overwrite the
object.

Resolved in commit `5fa01b0` (`Align POST object policy conditions with AWS`):
the POST Object path now passes `If-Match` / `If-None-Match` into bucket-policy
context without setting `s3:ObjectCreationOperation` and without converting them
into write conditions. AWS-facing s3-tests were added to lock down the behavior:

- `test_post_object_bucket_policy_object_creation_operation_condition_is_absent`
- `test_post_object_bucket_policy_if_none_match_header_is_policy_only`
- `test_post_object_bucket_policy_if_none_match_string_equals`
- `test_post_object_bucket_policy_if_match_string_equals_is_policy_only`

Validation run:
`./scripts/aws-tests --test post_object test_post_object_bucket_policy -- --nocapture`
passed all four tests against AWS.

